		description = "International police organization",
		tags = { "International organizations", "Governmental organizations" },
		aliases = { "The International Criminal Police Organization – INTERPOL", "ICPO–INTERPOL" },
		summary = "**The International Criminal Police Organization – INTERPOL** (**ICPO–INTERPOL**), commonly known as **Interpol**, is an international organization that facilitates worldwide police cooperation and crime control. It is the world's largest international police organization. It is headquartered in [[Lyon]], France, with seven regional bureaus worldwide and a National Central Bureau in each of its 196 member states.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The organization today known as Interpol was founded on 7 September 1923 at the close of a five-day International Police Congress in [[Vienna]] as the **International Criminal Police Commission** (**ICPC**). It adopted many of its current duties throughout the 1930s. The organization remained largely inactive until the end of [[World War II]]. In 1956, the ICPC adopted a new constitution and the name Interpol, derived from its telegraphic address used since 1946. The agency also facilitates cooperation among national law enforcement institutions through the use of criminal databases, crime information centers, and communications networks. Contrary to popular belief, Interpol is not itself a law enforcement agency. Interpol has an annual budget of €142 million ($155 million), most of which comes from annual contributions by its member police forces in 181 countries.",
